{
  "anomaly_detected": false,
  "confidence": 0.72,
  "anomalies": [],
  "summary": "The netlist and SPICE deck are internally consistent and match the specification's described architecture in every checked detail: the 24V input path (H12→FB5→F2 fuse→D3 TVS→T4 P-FET reverse-polarity→+24V), the TPS62933 buck (R37/R26 divider → 4.98V, L2, BST/SS/RT caps) and AZ1117 LDO rails, the main +3V3 I2C bus (R50/R17 2.2k pull-ups) with the EZO-RTD directly on it, the two ADM3260 isolated pH/EC channels with their isoPower VDD_ISO/GND_ISO domains and 2.2k isolated pull-ups, the three TMC2209 drivers (shared UART via R21 on IO21/IO22, individual STEP/EN/PDIS lines matching the spec's pin table exactly, 110mΩ sense resistors and 10.7k/14k VREF dividers), and the pump/valve low-side switches with their float-switch interlocks. Reference designators in the netlist are shifted relative to the spec (e.g. M5=ESP32, U9/U11=ADM3260, U3/U6/U8=TMC2209, U2=buck, U10=LDO, T8=IRLR2905, T4=AON6407, D3=TVS, F2=fuse), but this is the documented r1→r2 renumbering and the connectivity matches the spec's pin table and topology. Every passive's value, library part, footprint and description agree with its role, and the SPICE deck agrees with the netlist on all modelled components. The EZO-RTD being powered from +3V3 (its datasheet nominally wants 4.5–5.5V) is the only electrically questionable item, but the spec's own description treats the main non-isolated bus as the +3V3-referenced side (its headers break out +3V3) and never lists the RTD under +5V, so this build matches the intended reference rather than deviating from it. No anomaly is supported by the files."
}

